{% if ctx.slot_type == 'material' %}
<big-material>
This is a MATERIAL: the long feature the blog runs twice a week, built around a live theme.
It is not a news note and not a claim-check of one product - it is the piece someone sends
to a friend because it finally explains the whole thing.

Shape it as a real feature, in plain language for a reader with no background:
- Open on why this is worth a long read right now - a moment, a thing everyone half-knows
  and gets wrong. If you open on a number or a fact, give the reader one line of human
  footing first: a statistic dropped cold, with nobody saying it, reads like a documentary
  voiceover, not like you talking to one person.
- Tell it with an arc. Where a theme has a history, walk it: how it started, how it grew,
  where it stands now. Then what is being done about it - the resistance, the fixes, the
  open fights - and what it means for the reader specifically.
- Carry the evidence the whole way: every claim keeps its source with a URL, and where the
  data is thin or contested, say so plainly. On a theme this size, naming what is NOT known
  is part of the honesty.
- Sections with real subheadings that name the idea, not the category. Let it breathe -
  {{ type_cfg.min_words }}-{{ type_cfg.max_words }} words - but never pad; length is earned
  by having more to say, not by saying it slower.
- Land on a position, not a summary.
</big-material>

{% endif %}
{% if ctx.slot_type == 'roundup' %}
<friday-roundup>
This is the Friday post: what happened this week on one theme, gathered in one place.
Not five unrelated news items in a list — one theme, and the week's findings inside it.

- Open by naming the theme and why this week in particular is worth stopping on.
- Three to five items. Each gets a paragraph or two: what was published, by whom, in which
  journal, and — the part the press release leaves out — what it does not show.
- Order by what matters to the reader, never chronologically.
- Say plainly where the week's findings disagree with each other. A week in which the
  evidence pulled in two directions is a more honest post than one pretending it did not.
- Close on what you would actually change, or on why you are changing nothing yet.
- Every item carries a date, a named source and a URL.
- Do not pad to reach five. Three real items beat five with two fillers.
</friday-roundup>

{% endif %}
<requirements>
- Content type: {{ ctx.slot_type }}
- Word count: {{ type_cfg.min_words }}-{{ type_cfg.max_words }} words
- Language: Ukrainian
- MUST include source URLs for every health claim
- CITE WITH NUMBERS. In the body, mark each source with a bracketed number at the point of
  the claim, in order of first appearance: "мета-аналіз у The Lancet показав X [1]". Reuse
  the same number if you cite the same source again. Do NOT use markdown links for sources
  and do NOT add your own "## Джерела" section - the page builds the numbered list itself.
- Provide THREE PARALLEL ARRAYS in that exact [n] order: source_urls (the URL), source_names
  (short label: author/journal/year, e.g. "Matthias et al., Vaccine, 2007"), and
  source_descriptions (one plain line - what it is and what it found). Array item i is
  citation number i+1. Keep all three the same length.
- Links to YOUR OWN earlier posts (longlife.media/...) are the exception: those stay as
  inline markdown links, and are NOT numbered sources.
- Slug: latin lowercase with hyphens, descriptive, SEO-friendly
- Tags: Ukrainian topic tags relevant to the article
- Category: one of: nutrition, fitness, mental-health, sleep, longevity, biohacking, research, prevention
- Hashtags: topic hashtags (#здоровя, #харчування, #сон, #фітнес, #дослідження, etc.)

HEALTH CONTENT RULES (critical):
1. CITE SCIENTIFIC SOURCES: every claim about health effects must reference a study
   - "Дослідження у журналі The Lancet (2024) показало..." — with URL
   - "За даними мета-аналізу Cochrane..." — with URL
   - Note study type: "рандомізоване контрольоване дослідження", "мета-аналіз", "спостережне дослідження"
2. EXPLAIN MEDICAL TERMS: first mention always with simple explanation
   - "мелатонін — гормон, який регулює цикл сну та неспання"
   - "кортизол — так званий гормон стресу"
   - "VO2 max — максимальне споживання кисню, показник витривалості"
3. BALANCED CLAIMS: never absolute, always nuanced
   - "Дослідження показує..." NOT "Доведено, що..."
   - "Для більшості людей..." NOT "Для всіх..."
   - "Хоча результати обнадійливі, потрібні додаткові дослідження"
   - Include study limitations: sample size, population, duration
4. NO MEDICAL ADVICE: inform, don't prescribe
   - "Перед змінами в раціоні проконсультуйся з лікарем"
   - Never: "Прийми 500 мг вітаміну D щодня" — instead: "Рекомендована доза за МОЗ..."
5. PRACTICAL, BUT NOT FORMULAIC: where the evidence supports doing something differently,
   say what and for whom. Do not bolt a "Що можна зробити вже сьогодні" list onto every
   post — some findings do not translate into an action yet, and pretending otherwise is
   the single most common way health writing overstates itself. When that is the case,
   say so and end on the open question instead.
6. NO SCAREMONGERING:
   - "Цей продукт може підвищувати ризик..." NOT "Цей продукт вбиває!"
   - Always provide context: absolute vs. relative risk
   - If a study is alarming, note its limitations

